# Every variable marked REQUIRED is checked when the server starts
# (apps/server/src/config/environment.ts): missing or malformed ones abort the
# boot with a message naming the variable, instead of failing later as a 403 or
# a 500 that looks like a client bug.

# REQUIRED. Postgres (Supabase project, used only as a plain Postgres host — prefer session pooler for broad compatibility)
# Encode special characters in the password: & %26, $ %24, @ %40
# NOTE: whether this connection actually negotiates TLS has NOT been verified in
# this repo. Supabase's poolers accept TLS, and appending "?sslmode=require"
# should demand it, but nothing here proves the driver honours it end to end —
# treat that as an open item rather than a settled fact.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.[PROJECT_REF]:[PASSWORD]@aws-0-ap-southeast-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# Better Auth
# REQUIRED in production (sessions are signed with it). openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""
# REQUIRED in production: this deployment's own public origin, e.g. https://app.example.com.
# It is also the allow-list the same-origin check and the WebSocket upgrade guard
# use, so a wrong value rejects every mutation and every realtime subscription
# with a 403 while reads keep working.
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3072"
# Optional, comma-separated: extra origins to trust alongside BETTER_AUTH_URL (e.g. a tunnel host).
# Each entry must be an absolute http(s) URL.
BETTER_AUTH_EXTRA_ORIGINS=""

# apps/web (Vite) — must be prefixed VITE_ to be exposed to the browser bundle
VITE_BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3072"

# Production port for apps/server's combined API + static server. Optional (default 3072).
PORT=3072

# Dev only: the port apps/server binds to under `bun run dev`, and the port
# apps/web's Vite proxy forwards /api and /ws to. Optional (default 3073).
API_PORT=3073

# Optional: debug | info | warn | error. Default: info.
# At `info` you get committed commands, socket open/close, and every failure.
# `debug` additionally reports each bot drain and each broadcast's recipient
# count — the two things to turn on when realtime looks dead or a bot looks
# stuck, and the two that are too chatty to leave on (the room bootstrap is
# polled every ~5s per client and kicks a drain each time).
LOG_LEVEL="info"

# Bots — pause (ms) taken BEFORE each bot command, including the first of a
# chain, so a human sees bot turns land one at a time instead of all at once.
# Optional (default 1500).
#
# DO NOT LOWER THIS WITHOUT READING THE ARITHMETIC. It looks like a "feel" knob
# and it is not: it is one half of a budget whose other half lives in the client,
# and turning it down does not make the game faster, it makes the game
# unfollowable. It was 900 and that was measurably too fast.
#
#   Committing a bot turn is atomic. One turn.roll produces the roll, the move,
#   every resource change, any card draw, the tile resolution and the turn
#   hand-off as ONE batch of event summaries sharing ONE occurredAt timestamp.
#   Raising this value does not spread those events out — nothing on the server
#   can, short of splitting the engine command, which would change the rules.
#   The client is what spreads them: its event presentation queue plays a
#   committed turn out at ~180-320ms per event (see apps/web/src/lib/motion.ts).
#
#   A bot turn carries roughly 2-6 events, so it takes ~0.7-1.9s to render, with
#   a typical four-event turn near 1.0-1.3s. 1500 therefore sits just above the
#   top of that typical band: the next bot's events arrive a few hundred
#   milliseconds AFTER the previous turn has finished rendering. That gap is the
#   whole point — it is the beat in which a player registers what happened.
#
#   Set it materially lower and the beat disappears: the client's queue goes into
#   permanent backlog, the queue rather than this value becomes the pacer, and
#   the original symptom ("five log lines appear at once, I genuinely cannot
#   follow the game") returns with added latency on top. Set it materially higher
#   and a full six-seat table spends ten seconds a round watching nothing.
#
# If you change the client's per-event budget, change this too. They are two
# halves of one number.
#
# 0 switches the pause off completely and is fully supported (tests, load work, a
# deliberately fast local loop) — not a degraded mode, just "no pacing", with the
# burst behaviour above as its documented consequence.
#
# Must be a whole non-negative number of milliseconds, at most 10000. Anything
# else is reported as bots.turn-delay-invalid at warn level rather than taking
# the server down: garbage falls back to the default, and a value over the
# ceiling is CLAMPED to 10000 rather than sped back up (a mistyped 15000 in a
# six-seat room is a 75-second room-wide stall, because the pause is held inside
# the bot driver's per-room drain slot and the turn-timeout driver awaits that
# drain).
BOT_TURN_DELAY_MS=1500

# Turn timer — how many SECONDS a player gets before the server takes their turn
# for them. Optional (default 60).
#
# 0 switches the clock off completely, and that is a fully supported
# configuration rather than a degraded one: a turn timer is hostile in a casual
# game. With 0 no deadline is armed, none is projected to clients, and nothing is
# ever auto-committed on a player's behalf.
#
# Enforcement is server-side and does not depend on any client honouring the
# countdown: when the deadline passes, the server commits the absent player's
# turn itself (a roll, or — for an open prompt — the option that cannot cost them
# anything). Clients receive an absolute ISO deadline plus the duration and count
# down locally, so the value below is also what a player sees on screen.
#
# Must be a whole number of seconds and at least 5; anything else (a fraction, a
# negative, a non-number, 1..4) is reported as turn-timer.timeout-invalid at warn
# level and falls back to the default or the 5s floor rather than taking the
# server down. Below 5s a timer stops being a timer and becomes a way to lose
# your turn.
#
# Interaction with BOT_TURN_DELAY_MS: none, deliberately. A bot on turn is never
# on the clock, so a bot chain — however long its pauses make it — runs with no
# deadline armed at all, and the SAME write that hands the turn back to a human
# arms that human's deadline. So a player always gets the full budget from the
# moment their turn actually begins, no matter how slowly the bots played. This
# is the reason the bot pause is taken before each command rather than after:
# a trailing pause would advance the clock after the deadline was armed and
# quietly bill it to the player. Both properties are pinned by
# apps/server/tests/rooms/bot-turn-pacing.test.ts.
TURN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
